yone's  faces as they're looking around Damon  Allison Kristen and Joffrey sir Gerald  does come back up to accuse Damon of  killing his wife but he gets pretty much  shut down we missed an opportunity there  to see two Warriors go up against each  other who both wield a Valerian steel  blade Damon has dark sister and house  Royce has lamentation we see Damon watch  renera like the creepy inappropriate  Uncle he is and he catches lady Lenor  looking at him he goes to her and he  opens with a line that was attributed to  Mushroom in the book where he says has  anyone ever told you that you're nearly  as pretty as your brother and there does  seem to be some chemistry going on  between these two characters in the  middle of everything Joffrey which is  not the best name to have when you're  thinking about a royal wedding in  Westeros thinks that he's figured out  Kristen is Ramirez Paramore so like you  do he just walks right up to him and  lets him know to his credit he thinks  their interests align and that he's  making a pack that will be beneficial to  everyone but as we'll learn later his  timing couldn't have been worse we see  Damon cut into a dance between rhaenyra  and Harwin strong she kind of outdamens  Damon by daring him to cut through the  kingsguard and take her to Dragonstone  to make her his wife he sort of grabs  her face as viserys looks on which  serves as a reminder that there is  Passion between these two that wasn't  just a fluke at the brothel then  everything gets interrupted did when  Kristen Cole beats Joffrey to death in  front of everyone apparently he snapped  Leonor even gets caught up in it we see  Harwin jump in to save rhaenyra but only  after his father tells him to do it and  yeah he just beats his face in  completely then no one stops him when he  walks out of the room I suppose this  ruins everything so they call in the  septon and he marries them that night  rather than waiting for the full week of  tourneys and everything else that was  supposed to happen so Kristen goes to  the godswood to kill himself but Alison  stops him and then during the wedding as  they're exchanging their vows with Lenor  essentially fighting through his  heartache because his lover just died  once again we see viserys pass out on  the floor then the episode comes to an  end as we see a rat come up to take a  taste of the blood that Joffrey Left  Behind compared to episode 4 I was a  little mixed on this one lore wise there  was a lot to like and as much as I never  cared much for Alison as a care  character in the book her arrival at the  wedding here was fantastic following  after Damon where you're still stuck in  the what the hell is he doing here mode  made this all the more memorable plus  high tide was really interesting Corliss  has collected all this strange stuff  from his adventures on the negative side  I think they leaned a little too far  into playing with our expectations  surrounding a westerosi wedding and even  though they set it up so everything  would make sense I felt that some of the  Kristen stuff it just felt contrived  they compressed some things in order to  have everything happen at this  celebration and after watching it the  first time I was just sort of like oh  okay that's a way you could bring things  together I guess I think the desired  effect was wow I didn't see that coming  and hell yeah we're getting back into  some real Game of Thrones action but the  green wedding didn't rise to the heights  of some of the other ones we associate  with colors and it felt like they were  trying to stoke my expectation and that  something was about to happen so much  that once it did actually come it was  just sort of like oh is that it the one  thing that makes sense in taking this  route though is it puts him in a place  where Allison has to rescue him he could  very well lose everything I mean that's  why he went to the godswood to take his  own life and her rescuing him will come  with the price of her basically owning  him which depending on how they play  that could lead to some interesting  situations because he is someone who  represents something that raniera cared  about at one time at least I do like the  emphasis on the messy nature of all  these unions that are made in the  interest of preserving power though  we've gotten to see some of the cases  where it worked out with viserys and  Emma and Corliss and rhaenys so you can  see where they wouldn't have that much  interest in changing the system and I  particularly liked rainey's calling  Corliss out on his motivations because  even though he's saying he's going to do  what's necessary to write the Injustice  she suffered she's moved on and knows  that it's more about power and wealth  for this man that she clearly loves  anyways we've seen the opposite end of  things with Damon and Ria which is funny  because that all seems to stem from her  being able to see through his BS to  their credit raniera and Lenor seem to  have a good plan to make the most out of  their situation and that made the somber  situation of their wedding and  especially his vows hit pretty hard also  as low born as Kristen Cole is in  relation to this person that he's  entered into this forbidden relationship  with it's not terribly surprising that  he would miss how she would never give  that up and then we have to ask  ourselves is this the way he would react  or is it common enough he should have  actually realized that hey this isn't  that bad of a situation the strongs are  some of my favorite characters in this  part of the story so it was fun to see  Laris working the situation with Allison  and the conversation between Lionel and  viserys I'm not not sure if their  intention is for us to suspect Laris as  the one who sent the tea or they're just  showing that he has those varus or  little finger characteristics of being  able to go in and find the weak link and  get the information off of different  people in different positions but I know  a lot of people were wondering if the  king actually sent that so this could be  something towards your answer a pretty  solid episode besides the somewhat  confusing and underwhelming ending and a  pretty solid first half of the season to  this point they've made these characters  far more compelling than they were in  the book without really changing their  nature I think that bodes well for the  future since the story does change gears  once the characters get older it's been  an interesting look at how they become  who they'll be I'm looking forward to  seeing how things play out and I think  that's a good place to leave things let  me know in the comments what you thought  about this episode and the first half of  the season if you haven't read the book  where do you think things are headed it  and if you have how do you feel about  some of the changes and what we're  learning about these characters compared  to what we knew from reading the book  book spoilers are fine please just use  LINE breaks to hide those under the fold  when you comment so that they won't  spoil anything for people who haven't  read ahead please like this video If you  enjoyed it please subscribe to my  channel if you haven't already and  thanks for watching I'll talk to you  soon


